---
// Contribute: how lessons and problems are written and checked.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Contribute" description="How Inferview lessons and problems are written and checked." active="none">
  <div class="wrap page narrow prose">
    <h1>Contribute</h1>
    <p>Lessons, problems and labs are MDX files with checked frontmatter. Here is the format, so you can draft one.</p>

    <h2>A problem</h2>
    <p>One file per problem at <code>src/content/problems/&lt;track&gt;/&lt;id&gt;.mdx</code>, where the id is a three-letter track code and a number, such as <code>agt-002</code>. The frontmatter sets the type, difficulty, seniority (senior, staff or both) and minutes, plus what the type needs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>mcq and debug: three to five <code>choices</code> with exactly one correct.</li>
      <li>estimate: an accepted <code>range</code>.</li>
      <li>code: <code>code.starter</code>, <code>code.tests</code>, <code>code.solution</code> and optional <code>code.hidden</code>, tests that run after the visible ones and are never shown.</li>
      <li>multistage: <code>code.starter</code> and <code>code.solution</code>, and three or four <code>stages</code>, each with a title, a brief, tests and optional hidden tests. Only stage 1 has a starter; later stages continue from the reader's code.</li>
      <li>repo: <code>code.files</code> (path to content, at least two files), <code>code.target</code> (the file the reader edits), <code>code.tests</code>, optional <code>code.hidden</code>, and <code>code.solution</code> (the whole fixed target file).</li>
      <li>replay: a <code>replay</code> timeline of events; at least two events carry <code>choices</code> with exactly one correct, and the reader picks before the next event shows.</li>
      <li>critique, workflow, worksample and presentation: a <code>checklist</code> of 4 to 12 points the reader ticks.</li>
      <li>concept, design and behavioural: no extra fields; the Answer opens with a <code>&lt;Depth&gt;</code> block.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The body has the sections Question, Hints, Answer, Follow-ups and Common mistakes, in that order. <code>npm run code</code> runs every code, multistage and repo solution against its visible and hidden tests in local Python.</p>

    <h2>A lesson</h2>
    <p>Each module holds an ordered list of lessons at <code>src/content/lessons/&lt;track&gt;/&lt;module&gt;/&lt;lesson-slug&gt;.mdx</code>, with <code>order</code> setting the position inside the module, <code>kind</code> one of lesson, teardown, playbook or paper, and <code>level</code> senior or staff. A lesson opens on the decision or mechanism, works it with numbers, carries a widget or a prediction, real numbers with sources, short runnable code, one interview question answered at three depths, and the limits of the lesson's model of the world. A teardown draws the system with <code>&lt;Diagram&gt;</code> and walks a request with <code>&lt;Trace&gt;</code>.</p>

    <h2>A lab</h2>
    <p>One file per lab at <code>src/content/labs/&lt;lab-slug&gt;.mdx</code>, with 5 to 10 steps, or 3 to 10 stages with <code>kind: multistage</code>. Each step has a title, a brief, starter code, tests, optional hidden tests and a solution, and its tests run against the program built in the steps before it. <code>npm run labs</code> runs every step's tests against the reference solutions in local Python. A new lab slug also goes into <code>labSlugs()</code> and <code>validLabs()</code> in <code>firestore.rules</code>.</p>

    <h2>The rules that are checked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every number, quote or borrowed example has an entry in <code>sources</code> with a title and a URL.</li>
      <li>A company appears in <code>companies</code> only with a public URL that names the company and the topic.</li>
      <li>No em dashes, and no en dashes used as dashes.</li>
      <li>Track and module slugs must exist, the problem id must match its file name, a lesson's folder must be a module of its track, and lesson order is unique inside a module.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><code>npm run validate</code> checks all of this in a second, without a full build.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
